From abb3e31d4f31d69172b40bb7dfae49ccfae6b99c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: irregularthanis <343663069+irregularthanis@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 13:12:13 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 195/196] GXRuntime: guest alias lookup in constant time (last
 hit, then a page index)

ppc_guest_alias_resolve binary-searched the sorted alias index on every
access to REL data: with Wind Waker's ~1,900 aliases, about 11 dependent
loads per guest load or store. Where the game thread walks REL data (the
lava room's collision objects on a Steam Deck), that was 10% of the thread.

While no two aliases overlap:
- the alias the last lookup found is tried first (RecompCore #34): at most
  one alias holds an access, so if it does it is the answer; a relaxed
  atomic, as a thread-local costs a call per access in a Mach-O dylib;
- otherwise a page index, rebuilt by the mutators like the sorted index,
  gives the search's starting position for the address's 4 KiB page, and a
  short forward scan reaches the same position the search would.
Overlapping sets keep the registration-order scan; a span wider than 32 MiB
keeps the binary search. Results are unchanged for every input.

BLUEWAKE_TRACE_ALIAS_COST adds last_hits, page_lookups and page_steps, and
ppc_guest_alias_cost_report_now() prints the census for hosts that exit
without this library's atexit handlers.

tests/guest_alias_lookup_test.c checks the resolver against the
registration-order rule for 11.2 million accesses (a Wind Waker-like layout,
removes and re-adds, 4,096 aliases, page edges, sizes 1-8, a span over
32 MiB, overlapping sets), in both BW_GUEST_MEM1 variants.

Planned in BlueWake docs/planning/2026-10-29-alias_resolver_lookup.
Stacked on #45.
